{% macro multiple_annotators(examples, marker, legacy_aliases=false) -%}
{% if legacy_aliases %}
.. _classification-with-multiple-annotators:
.. _pool-based-active-learning-multi-annotator-learning:

{% endif %}
{{ heading("Single-output Classification with Multiple Annotators", marker) }}

Each sample has one class label to predict. Several annotators can provide
annotations for it, and the strategy selects sample–annotator pairs.
The animation combines classification utilities and decision boundaries
with a chart of requests per annotator.

.. image:: /examples/pool_multi_annotator_legend.png
   :alt: Legend for class labels, annotator correctness, decision boundaries, and utility scores.

{{ gallery(examples["pool-multi-annotator"]) }}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro pool_classification(examples, marker, legacy_aliases=false) -%}
{% set query_marker = "~" if marker == "-" else '"' %}
{% if legacy_aliases %}
.. _single-output-classification:
.. _pool-based-active-learning-classification:

{% endif %}
{{ heading("Single-output Classification", marker) }}

One annotator supplies one class label per selected sample. The following
groups distinguish one sample per cycle from batches with several samples.

.. image:: /examples/pool_classification_legend.png
   :alt: Legend for class labels, decision boundaries, labeled samples, and utility scores.

{{ heading("Single-sample Queries", query_marker) }}

Each cycle acquires one sample. The animation shows acquisition utilities
and the classifier's decision boundary in one feature-space plot.

{{ gallery(examples["pool-classification"]) }}

{{ heading("Batch Queries", query_marker) }}

One annotator supplies one class label per selected sample. These animations
acquire several samples per cycle. The grid shows the utilities used for
successive selections within the batch; the same classifier and observed
labels apply to every panel.

{{ gallery(examples["pool-classification-batch"]) }}

{% if legacy_aliases %}
.. _multi-label-classification:

{% endif %}
{{ heading("Multi-label Classification", marker) }}

One annotator supplies a complete vector of binary labels for each selected
sample. These examples acquire one vector per cycle. The three panels share
acquisition utilities and show a separate decision boundary for each label.
In each panel, class 0 means that the label is absent and class 1 means that
it is present. Gray markers identify samples whose label vectors have been
acquired.

.. image:: /examples/pool_classification_legend.png
   :alt: Legend applied per label: binary label values, decision boundaries, acquired samples, and utility scores.

{{ gallery(examples["pool-multilabel"]) }}

{{ multiple_annotators(examples, marker, legacy_aliases) }}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro pool_regression(examples) -%}
One annotator supplies a continuous target value for each selected sample.
The two plots show the fitted regression curve and acquisition utilities.
Some examples acquire one sample per cycle; others acquire a batch. Both
use the same two plots for prediction and utilities.

.. image:: /examples/pool_regression_legend.png
   :alt: Legend for the regression curve, utility scores, and labeled and unlabeled samples.

{{ gallery(examples["pool-regression"]) }}
{%- endmacro %}

{% macro stream_classification(examples, marker) -%}
{{ heading("Single-output Classification", marker) }}

One annotator supplies one class label for each queried sample. Samples
arrive in sequence, and the strategy decides whether to request each
sample's label within its budget. The animation shows arriving samples and
the evolving decision boundary over time.

.. image:: /examples/stream_classification_legend.png
   :alt: Legend for class labels, unlabeled and current samples, and the decision boundary.

{{ gallery(examples["stream-classification"]) }}
{%- endmacro %}
